[[:db/retract 17592186152381 :question/answer-fields 17592186148817] {:db/id 17592186152224 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-ab77c5c3-a1c5-5710-a08d-82e66f9fdecd" "question-a3d67809-78b0-59dc-9828-ea082eca69bb" "question-93f4dbff-c62f-571f-9c49-e5503854925a"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186152034 :question/problem "![](images/5c/5ce1e5bc9489f54f405f96a74e4e02458149be011a952f91603d6cd22e9b0a01.svg)\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ and vector $\\mathbf v$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\mathbf{v}$ is an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda=4$ of $A$\n\nII. $\\mathbf{v}$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda=4$ of $A$\n\nIII. $\\mathbf{v}$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $1$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda=4$ of $A$" :question/worked-solution "A *generalized eigenvector* $\\mathbf{v_2}$ of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda$ of a matrix $A$ is a nonzero vector such that\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{v}_2 \\xrightarrow{A- \\lambda I} \\mathbf{v}_1 \\xrightarrow{A- \\lambda I} \\mathbf{0},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{v}_1 \\neq \\mathbf{0}.$ Recall that an *eigenvector* is simply a generalized eigenvector of rank $1.$ \n\nWith this in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\nFirst, we compute $A-4I{:}$\n![](images/07/07d28376d40c024d22c60085ccac3f600a057e45e624e522c815755aaf8fe8e6.svg)\n\n- Next, we find the image of $\\mathbf{v}$ under the action of $A-4I{:}$\n![](images/6d/6dcb4edb0b165ab337b54783c4f30015379224e1ee156876b459666cc760de81.svg)\n\n- Since we got a nonzero vector in the last step, we now compute the image of $\\mathbf{w}$ under the action of $A-4I{:}$\n![](images/42/42b6edeb0a13e90007c6c6f15a68440c3b7a00727654d6db0ce69f5b2bd65913.svg)\n\nSo, we obtain the following chain of maps:\n\n![](images/7c/7c4372cfe047a9f2ddaf90e15750975da6d562f5287aba2bfe414335dba56b42.svg)\n\nHere, we see that $A-4I$ maps $\\mathbf{v}$ to $\\mathbf{0}$ in $2$ steps.\n\nTherefore:\n\n$\\qquad$ *$\\mathbf{v}$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda=4$ of $A.$*\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186152225 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-c32d62b6-73f0-5c6b-b445-e1066fb0b96f"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186152035 :question/problem "$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\overset{A+3I\\,}{\\to }v\\overset{A+3I\\,}{\\to }w\\overset{A+3I\\,}{\\to }0 \\\\ x\\overset{A-5I\\,}{\\to }y\\overset{A-5I\\,}{\\to }0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven the action of the matrices $A+3I$ and $A-5I$ on the nonzero vectors $\\mathbf{u},\\mathbf{v},\\mathbf{w},\\mathbf{x}$ and $\\mathbf y$ as depicted above, which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\mathbf{v}$ is an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda=-3$ of $A$\n\nII. $\\mathbf{u}$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $3$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda=-3$ of $A$\n\nIII. $\\mathbf{y}$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda=5$ of $A$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186152226 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-72a34fdf-b3e5-500c-96f3-76e3e66d1191"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186152036 :question/problem "![](images/a4/a494e86e18d97ca9332738cc6d85582a71b6933bb6afe6621b83e675c2272fb9.svg)\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ and vector $\\mathbf v$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\mathbf{v}$ is an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda=1$ of $A$\n\nII. $\\mathbf{v}$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda=1$ of $A$\n\nIII. $\\mathbf{v}$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $3$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda=1$ of $A$" :question/worked-solution "A *generalized eigenvector* $\\mathbf{v_n}$ of rank $n$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda$ of a matrix $A$ is a nonzero vector such that\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{v}_n \\xrightarrow{A- \\lambda I} \\mathbf{v}_{n-1} \\xrightarrow{A- \\lambda I} \\ldots \\xrightarrow{A- \\lambda I} \\mathbf{v}_1 \\xrightarrow{A- \\lambda I} \\mathbf{0},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{v}_{n-1}, \\mathbf{v}_{n-2},\\ldots, \\mathbf{v}_{1} \\neq \\mathbf{0}.$ An *eigenvector* is simply a generalized eigenvector of rank $1.$\n\n- First, we compute $A-I{:}$\n\n![](images/ef/efe92eb57e67b1113330fecc7009cf59d3a06f0f5f33ac8e800804f0067ff9eb.svg)\n\n- Next, we find the image of $\\mathbf{v}$ under the action of $A-I{:}$\n![](images/3c/3ca24127a41102d7b4f5f7118620ccc88c3452cc10dfd68a2f38c4b767435342.svg)\n\n- Since we got a nonzero vector, we now compute the image of $\\mathbf{w}$ under the action of $A-I{:}$\n![](images/c9/c92357786ae2b400582794c004d4c0c582fa07fbd8822a8dbc1a871d953b72b2.svg)\n\n- Again, since we got a nonzero vector in the last step, we now compute the image of $\\mathbf{x}$ under the action of $A-I{:}$\n![](images/91/91fd2e0b4a21407faf575572ed915fc546b85edb03a7077417425bd1c2ee98e7.svg)\n\nSo, we obtain the following chain of maps:\n\n![](images/8b/8b6b7659b3c647858a709e6ae1b1ce482ab940bba9b2d01280da4bbe47c12fdd.svg)\n\nHere, we see that $A-I$ maps $\\mathbf{v}$ to $\\mathbf{0}$ in $3$ steps.\n\nTherefore:\n\n$\\qquad$ *$\\mathbf{v}$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $3$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda=1$ of $A.$*\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186246999 :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-5 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{aligned}],\\,v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ and vector $v$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $v$ is an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-4$ of $A$\n\nII. $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-4$ of $A$\n\nIII. $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $1$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-4$ of $A$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-ab77c5c3-a1c5-5710-a08d-82e66f9fdecd" :question/id #uuid "ab77c5c3-a1c5-5710-a08d-82e66f9fdecd" :question/math-academy-id "q-109488" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -8 \\\\ 2 & -5\\end{aligned}],\\,v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ and vector $v$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1$ of $A$\n\nII. $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $1$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1$ of $A$\n\nIII. $v$ is an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1$ of $A$" :question/worked-solution "A *generalized eigenvector* ${v}_{2}$ of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda $ of a matrix $A$ is a nonzero vector such that\n\n$$\n{v}_{2}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\nwhere ${v}_{1}\\ne 0.$ Recall that an *eigenvector* is simply a generalized eigenvector of rank $1.$\n\nWith this in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\nFirst, we compute $A+I:$\n\n$$\nA+I=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -8 \\\\ 2 & -5\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -8 \\\\ 2 & -4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- Next, we find the image of $v$ under the action of $A+I:$\n\n$$\n(A+I)v=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -8 \\\\ 2 & -4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]={{[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{w}\\ne 0\n$$\n\n- Since we got a nonzero vector in the last step, we now compute the image of $w$ under the action of $A+I:$\n\n$$\n(A+I)w=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -8 \\\\ 2 & -4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=0\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain the following chain of maps:\n\n$$\n{{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{v}\\overset{A+I\\,}{\\to }{{[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{w}\\overset{A+I\\,}{\\to }{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{0}\n$$\n\nHere, we see that $A+I$ maps $v$ to $0$ in $2$ steps.\n\nTherefore:\n\n$\\,$ *$v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1$ of $A.$*\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-148bd3ea-4973-557b-89b5-48219c1a378a"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-148bd3ea-4973-557b-89b5-48219c1a378a" :answer-field/id #uuid "148bd3ea-4973-557b-89b5-48219c1a378a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-72e1debb-2d7b-595f-85d2-469a31d07a3a" :answer-field/choices ["answer-b5fda644-47ed-58ee-9a8b-8a80fc0d503b" "answer-72e1debb-2d7b-595f-85d2-469a31d07a3a" "answer-58792cac-2b0d-543d-8e9e-c8057305744f" "answer-4123c97a-32f0-5090-ad8f-278e9a0415cb" "answer-fb4e6251-9cc8-5873-b9c2-aab8505ef2cd"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b5fda644-47ed-58ee-9a8b-8a80fc0d503b" :answer/id #uuid "b5fda644-47ed-58ee-9a8b-8a80fc0d503b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-72e1debb-2d7b-595f-85d2-469a31d07a3a" :answer/id #uuid "72e1debb-2d7b-595f-85d2-469a31d07a3a" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-58792cac-2b0d-543d-8e9e-c8057305744f" :answer/id #uuid "58792cac-2b0d-543d-8e9e-c8057305744f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4123c97a-32f0-5090-ad8f-278e9a0415cb" :answer/id #uuid "4123c97a-32f0-5090-ad8f-278e9a0415cb" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fb4e6251-9cc8-5873-b9c2-aab8505ef2cd" :answer/id #uuid "fb4e6251-9cc8-5873-b9c2-aab8505ef2cd" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186244298 :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,v=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ and vector $v$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $1$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =3$ of $A$\n\nII. $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =3$ of $A$\n\nIII. $v$ is an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =3$ of $A$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-a3d67809-78b0-59dc-9828-ea082eca69bb" :question/id #uuid "a3d67809-78b0-59dc-9828-ea082eca69bb" :question/math-academy-id "q-109476" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,v=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ and vector $v$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $v$ is an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1$ of $A$\n\nII. $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1$ of $A$\n\nIII. $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $1$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1$ of $A$" :question/worked-solution "A *generalized eigenvector* ${v}_{2}$ of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda $ of a matrix $A$ is a nonzero vector such that\n\n$$\n{v}_{2}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\nwhere ${v}_{1}\\ne 0.$ Recall that an *eigenvector* is simply a generalized eigenvector of rank $1.$\n\nWith this in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\nFirst, we compute $A+I:$\n\n$$\nA+I=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- Next, we find the image of $v$ under the action of $A+I:$\n\n$$\n(A+I)v=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]={{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{w}\\ne 0\n$$\n\n- Since we got a nonzero vector in the last step, we now compute the image of $w$ under the action of $A+I:$\n\n$$\n(A+I)w=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=0\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain the following chain of maps:\n\n$$\n{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{v}\\overset{A+I\\,}{\\to }{{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{w}\\overset{A+I\\,}{\\to }{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{0}\n$$\n\nHere, we see that $A+I$ maps $v$ to $0$ in $2$ steps.\n\nTherefore:\n\n$\\,$ *$v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1$ of $A.$*\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-1a5f1e17-ee64-5f88-acce-1fb89abad6f0"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-1a5f1e17-ee64-5f88-acce-1fb89abad6f0" :answer-field/id #uuid "1a5f1e17-ee64-5f88-acce-1fb89abad6f0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-ebc1826a-9a0c-57f0-84eb-fb71a37f6300" :answer-field/choices ["answer-e31b4d52-32a8-5673-be5d-c721da9231aa" "answer-23feb840-9ea0-5b4a-9981-47d41adc532e" "answer-ef393691-7df5-5626-987f-128f055c264d" "answer-ebc1826a-9a0c-57f0-84eb-fb71a37f6300" "answer-31251623-175e-5172-b53d-93dabfd51773"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e31b4d52-32a8-5673-be5d-c721da9231aa" :answer/id #uuid "e31b4d52-32a8-5673-be5d-c721da9231aa" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-23feb840-9ea0-5b4a-9981-47d41adc532e" :answer/id #uuid "23feb840-9ea0-5b4a-9981-47d41adc532e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ef393691-7df5-5626-987f-128f055c264d" :answer/id #uuid "ef393691-7df5-5626-987f-128f055c264d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ebc1826a-9a0c-57f0-84eb-fb71a37f6300" :answer/id #uuid "ebc1826a-9a0c-57f0-84eb-fb71a37f6300" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-31251623-175e-5172-b53d-93dabfd51773" :answer/id #uuid "31251623-175e-5172-b53d-93dabfd51773" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-93f4dbff-c62f-571f-9c49-e5503854925a" :question/id #uuid "93f4dbff-c62f-571f-9c49-e5503854925a" :question/math-academy-id "q-109518" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,v=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ and vector $v$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =1$ of $A$\n\nII. $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $1$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =1$ of $A$\n\nIII. $v$ is an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =1$ of $A$" :question/worked-solution "A *generalized eigenvector* ${v}_{2}$ of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda $ of a matrix $A$ is a nonzero vector such that\n\n$$\n{v}_{2}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\nwhere ${v}_{1}\\ne 0.$ Recall that an *eigenvector* is simply a generalized eigenvector of rank $1.$\n\nWith this in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\nFirst, we compute $A-I:$\n\n$$\nA-I=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -1\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- Next, we find the image of $v$ under the action of $A-I:$\n\n$$\n(A-I)v=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]={{[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{w}\\ne 0\n$$\n\n- Since we got a nonzero vector in the last step, we now compute the image of $w$ under the action of $A-I:$\n\n$$\n(A-I)w=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=0\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain the following chain of maps:\n\n$$\n{{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{v}\\overset{A-I\\,}{\\to }{{[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{w}\\overset{A-I\\,}{\\to }{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{0}\n$$\n\nHere, we see that $A-I$ maps $v$ to $0$ in $2$ steps.\n\nTherefore:\n\n$\\,$ *$v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =1$ of $A.$*\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-263fbd38-e795-53f6-b66f-cb287c466369"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-263fbd38-e795-53f6-b66f-cb287c466369" :answer-field/id #uuid "263fbd38-e795-53f6-b66f-cb287c466369" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-d1cec6f6-74c4-595e-b9a4-e8d9615d9af5" :answer-field/choices ["answer-5fc5b2e1-1001-57bc-9ebf-e1899b27c976" "answer-d1cec6f6-74c4-595e-b9a4-e8d9615d9af5" "answer-16957b53-7f2c-5897-8254-366068b7d73a" "answer-974e648d-43b7-5056-bead-1b5c05bf7686" "answer-698b7655-84cd-57ff-b48c-076442522213"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5fc5b2e1-1001-57bc-9ebf-e1899b27c976" :answer/id #uuid "5fc5b2e1-1001-57bc-9ebf-e1899b27c976" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d1cec6f6-74c4-595e-b9a4-e8d9615d9af5" :answer/id #uuid "d1cec6f6-74c4-595e-b9a4-e8d9615d9af5" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-16957b53-7f2c-5897-8254-366068b7d73a" :answer/id #uuid "16957b53-7f2c-5897-8254-366068b7d73a" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-974e648d-43b7-5056-bead-1b5c05bf7686" :answer/id #uuid "974e648d-43b7-5056-bead-1b5c05bf7686" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-698b7655-84cd-57ff-b48c-076442522213" :answer/id #uuid "698b7655-84cd-57ff-b48c-076442522213" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186244292 :question/problem "$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\overset{A+4I\\,}{\\to }v\\overset{A+4I\\,}{\\to }w\\overset{A+4I\\,}{\\to }0 \\\\ x\\overset{A+3I\\,}{\\to }y\\overset{A+3I\\,}{\\to }z\\overset{A+3I\\,}{\\to }0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven the action of the matrices $A+4I$ and $A+3I$ on the nonzero vectors $u,v,w,x,$ $y$ and $z$ as depicted above, which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $3$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-4$ of $A$\n\nII. $x$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $3$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-3$ of $A$\n\nIII. $y$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $1$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-3$ of $A$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186244253 :question/problem "$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\overset{A-6I\\,}{\\to }v\\overset{A-6I\\,}{\\to }w\\overset{A-6I\\,}{\\to }0 \\\\ x\\overset{A+7I\\,}{\\to }y\\overset{A+7I\\,}{\\to }z\\overset{A+7I\\,}{\\to }0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven the action of the matrices $A-6I$ and $A+7I$ on the nonzero vectors $u,v,w,x,$ $y$ and $z$ as depicted above, which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $w$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $3$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =6$ of $A$\n\nII. $x$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $3$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-7$ of $A$\n\nIII. $y$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-7$ of $A$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186244274 :question/problem "$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\overset{A+9I\\,}{\\to }v\\overset{A+9I\\,}{\\to }w\\overset{A+9I\\,}{\\to }0 \\\\ x\\overset{A-I\\,}{\\to }y\\overset{A-I\\,}{\\to }z\\overset{A-I\\,}{\\to }0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven the action of the matrices $A+9I$ and $A-I$ on the nonzero vectors $u,v,w,x,$ $y$ and $z$ as depicted above, which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $1$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-9$ of $A$\n\nII. $x$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =1$ of $A$\n\nIII. $z$ is an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =1$ of $A$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-c32d62b6-73f0-5c6b-b445-e1066fb0b96f" :question/id #uuid "c32d62b6-73f0-5c6b-b445-e1066fb0b96f" :question/math-academy-id "q-109242" :question/problem "$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\overset{A+2I\\,}{\\to }v\\overset{A+2I\\,}{\\to }w\\overset{A+2I\\,}{\\to }0 \\\\ x\\overset{A-2I\\,}{\\to }y\\overset{A-2I\\,}{\\to }0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven the action of the matrices $A+2I$ and $A-2I$ on the nonzero vectors $u,v,w,x$ and $y$ as depicted above, which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $v$ is an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-2$ of $A$\n\nII. $x$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $3$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2$ of $A$\n\nIII. $y$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $1$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2$ of $A$" :question/worked-solution "A *generalized eigenvector* ${v}_{n}$ of rank $n$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda $ of a matrix $A$ is a nonzero vector such that\n\n$$\n{v}_{n}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{n-1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }…\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\nwhere ${v}_{n-1},{v}_{n-2},…,{v}_{1}\\ne 0.$ An *eigenvector* is simply a generalized eigenvector of rank $1.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since \n\n$$\nv\\overset{A+2I\\,}{\\to }w,\n$$\n\nand $w\\ne 0,$  the vector $v$ is  **not** a eigenvector of $A$ corresponding  to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-2.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since\n\n$$\nx\\overset{A-2I\\,}{\\to }y\\overset{A-2I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\n \ni.e. $A-2I$ maps $x$ to $0$ in $2$ steps, \nthe vector $x$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2,$ not $3.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, since\n\n$$\ny\\overset{A-2I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\ni.e. $A-2I$ maps $y$ to $0$ in $1$ step, \nthe vector $y$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $1$ (a.k.a. an eigenvector) corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2$ of $A.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-59068b8c-c566-5e5f-a6b7-89faa773c3aa"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-59068b8c-c566-5e5f-a6b7-89faa773c3aa" :answer-field/id #uuid "59068b8c-c566-5e5f-a6b7-89faa773c3aa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-85817bc0-a638-5f2e-8f8a-cdeeff6b3d1e" :answer-field/choices ["answer-c9aab259-7b9a-54be-b6ec-554fd1c11b3c" "answer-5c580e8c-17f9-5329-b05e-162735ed92b2" "answer-71851cec-299e-55b4-b56b-b60fb7b9e7ea" "answer-5b612074-81a0-5d6f-aec5-96eeb7af6bb1" "answer-85817bc0-a638-5f2e-8f8a-cdeeff6b3d1e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c9aab259-7b9a-54be-b6ec-554fd1c11b3c" :answer/id #uuid "c9aab259-7b9a-54be-b6ec-554fd1c11b3c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5c580e8c-17f9-5329-b05e-162735ed92b2" :answer/id #uuid "5c580e8c-17f9-5329-b05e-162735ed92b2" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-71851cec-299e-55b4-b56b-b60fb7b9e7ea" :answer/id #uuid "71851cec-299e-55b4-b56b-b60fb7b9e7ea" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5b612074-81a0-5d6f-aec5-96eeb7af6bb1" :answer/id #uuid "5b612074-81a0-5d6f-aec5-96eeb7af6bb1" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-85817bc0-a638-5f2e-8f8a-cdeeff6b3d1e" :answer/id #uuid "85817bc0-a638-5f2e-8f8a-cdeeff6b3d1e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186244251 :question/problem "$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\overset{A-I\\,}{\\to }v\\overset{A-I\\,}{\\to }w\\overset{A-I\\,}{\\to }0 \\\\ x\\overset{A+4I\\,}{\\to }y\\overset{A+4I\\,}{\\to }z\\overset{A+4I\\,}{\\to }0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven the action of the matrices $A-I$ and $A+4I$ on the nonzero vectors $u,v,w,x,$ $y$ and $z$ as depicted above, which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $w$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =1$ of $A$\n\nII. $u$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $3$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =1$ of $A$\n\nIII. $y$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-4$ of $A$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186152381 :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 1 & -5 \\\\ -4 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}],\\,v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ and vector $v$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $v$ is an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-3$ of $A$\n\nII. $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $1$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-3$ of $A$\n\nIII. $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $3$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-3$ of $A$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186152384 :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -2 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 3 & -7\\end{aligned}],\\,v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ and vector $v$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $3$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-5$ of $A$\n\nII. $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $1$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-5$ of $A$\n\nIII. $v$ is an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-5$ of $A$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186247011 :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2\\end{aligned}],\\,v=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ and vector $v$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $3$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-2$ of $A$\n\nII. $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $1$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-2$ of $A$\n\nIII. $v$ is an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-2$ of $A$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-72a34fdf-b3e5-500c-96f3-76e3e66d1191" :question/id #uuid "72a34fdf-b3e5-500c-96f3-76e3e66d1191" :question/math-academy-id "q-109572" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ and vector $v$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $v$ is an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2$ of $A$\n\nII. $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $1$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2$ of $A$\n\nIII. $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $3$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2$ of $A$" :question/worked-solution "A *generalized eigenvector* ${v}_{n}$ of rank $n$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda $ of a matrix $A$ is a nonzero vector such that\n\n$$\n{v}_{n}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{n-1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }…\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\nwhere ${v}_{n-1},{v}_{n-2},…,{v}_{1}\\ne 0.$ An *eigenvector* is simply a generalized eigenvector of rank $1.$\n\nFirst, we compute $A-2I:$\n\n$$\nA-2I=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]-2[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- Next, we find the image of $v$ under the action of $A-2I:$\n\n$$\n(A-2I)v=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]={{[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{w}\\ne 0\n$$\n\n- Since we got a nonzero vector, we now compute the image of $w$ under the action of $A-2I:$\n\n$$\n(A-2I)w=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]={{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -8 \\\\ -8\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{x}\\ne 0\n$$\n\n- Again, since we got a nonzero vector in the last step, we now compute the image of $x$ under the action of $A-2I:$\n\n$$\n(A-2I)x=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -8 \\\\ -8\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=0\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain the following chain of maps:\n\n$$\n{{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{v}\\overset{A-2I\\,}{\\to }{{[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{w}\\overset{A-2I\\,}{\\to }{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -8 \\\\ -8\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{x}\\overset{A-2I\\,}{\\to }{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{0}\n$$\n\nHere, we see that $A-2I$ maps $v$ to $0$ in $3$ steps.\n\nTherefore:\n\n$\\,$ *$v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $3$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2$ of $A.$*\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-287f4509-af62-597e-a469-54e8dc29ab13"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-287f4509-af62-597e-a469-54e8dc29ab13" :answer-field/id #uuid "287f4509-af62-597e-a469-54e8dc29ab13" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-dbcccae4-29dd-5752-98f9-f75c35190636" :answer-field/choices ["answer-adc06e2a-797f-5b5b-b125-a1b83e296a09" "answer-68d2143e-ca23-5a4c-95d7-62217b46497f" "answer-0898420b-4c54-5e1d-97e3-5cc4bfdd8109" "answer-dbcccae4-29dd-5752-98f9-f75c35190636" "answer-6a0f6d67-6d78-567d-a365-5c0702b884e3"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-adc06e2a-797f-5b5b-b125-a1b83e296a09" :answer/id #uuid "adc06e2a-797f-5b5b-b125-a1b83e296a09" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-68d2143e-ca23-5a4c-95d7-62217b46497f" :answer/id #uuid "68d2143e-ca23-5a4c-95d7-62217b46497f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0898420b-4c54-5e1d-97e3-5cc4bfdd8109" :answer/id #uuid "0898420b-4c54-5e1d-97e3-5cc4bfdd8109" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-dbcccae4-29dd-5752-98f9-f75c35190636" :answer/id #uuid "dbcccae4-29dd-5752-98f9-f75c35190636" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6a0f6d67-6d78-567d-a365-5c0702b884e3" :answer/id #uuid "6a0f6d67-6d78-567d-a365-5c0702b884e3" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186362682 :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ and vector $v$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $v$ is an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1$ of $A$\n\nII. $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $1$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1$ of $A$\n\nIII. $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1$ of $A$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186153411 :tutorial/content "Consider the matrix $A$ and the vector $\\mathbf v,$ given by\n![](images/86/866c306428d7726f8078af9f608a839d6f4971a4a3e368e84d023503ea22ba82.svg)\nThe matrix $A$ has only one eigenvalue, namely $\\lambda = 3.$ Let's show that $\\mathbf v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to this eigenvalue.\n\n- First, we compute $A-3I{:}$\n![](images/b7/b7bcc3d7905efb5425f9ad2e840d574a0c613b3fbdd65742b24608d8f17fcf1b.svg)\n\n- Next, we find the image of $\\mathbf{v}$ under the action of $A-3I{:}$\n![](images/df/df135de6113520b548112c1b8cd9f77d51d99c9b1c5ee3d35f0ab4ce1f6f500e.svg)\n\n- Since we got a nonzero vector in the last step, we now compute the image of $\\mathbf{w}$ under the action of $A-3I{:}$\n![](images/d2/d24081710386fa3719b171bc47f5d10ff12ddecdd0f9fa5da6d5ef4926b9c3e3.svg)\n\nSo, we obtain the following chain of maps:\n\n![](images/90/9041ec615dce97097d94d895e6668e4b97096fd91ad47947fac7f7841c739c9d.svg)\n\nAs we can see,  $A-3I$ maps $\\mathbf{v}$ to $\\mathbf{0}$ in $2$ steps.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $\\mathbf{v}$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda=3$ of $A.$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186152358 :activity/expected-seconds 600.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]}]
